Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 23:48:15 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 072/154] feat(routing): hold the rules and restore the
 reentrancy bypass
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

routing.json becomes the fourth store the startup trip reads, for the reason the
other two joined it: the rules must be in hand before the first navigation can be
asked about, and a separate read would land on its own schedule. A launch that
armed routing before or after the first tab depending on disk timing is the kind
of order nothing asserts and everything depends on.

ScopedRoutingBypass is back. Every AddTabAt this controller makes is non-routed
by construction - it is a restore, a favourite, or the routed reopen itself,
which must not be evaluated twice. Without that last case two rules pointing at
each other's spaces bounce one tab between them forever, and no cycle detector
would be as cheap or as certain as not asking twice.

It restores rather than clears on scope exit, so a nested bypass cannot end the
outer one and expose the next navigation while a reopen is still in flight. That
is the half worth the test, and clearing instead fails it.

The startup tab is wrapped too. It used to be safe by ordering alone - it runs
before that host's routing wires - and not relying on that ordering is what the
old notes queued a bypass here for.

Nothing consults the rules yet: the three seams are the next piece.

Signed-off-by: János Mikó <693558+janosmiko@users.noreply.github.com>
